PMID- 37181330
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DP  - 2023 Jul
TI  - Cloud-based biomedical data storage and analysis for genomic research: Landscape
      analysis of data governance in emerging NIH-supported platforms.
PG  - 100196
LID - 10.1016/j.xhgg.2023.100196 [doi]
AB  - As genomic research increasingly moves to the cloud, understanding data
      governance across NIH-supported platforms is essential. We reviewed 94 publicly
      available documents from five NIH-funded cloud platforms (All of Us Research Hub,
      NHGRI AnVIL, NHLBI BioData Catalyst, NCI Genomic Data Commons, and Kids First
      Data Resource Center) and the dbGaP data sharing mechanism. Platforms were
      analyzed across seven categories of data governance: data submission, data
      ingestion, user authentication and authorization, data security, data access,
      auditing, and sanctions. Similarities across platforms included reliance on a
      formal data ingestion process, multiple tiers of data access with varying user
      authentication and/or authorization requirements, platform and user security
      measures, and auditing for inappropriate data use. Platforms differed in how data
      tiers were organized and in the specifics of user authentication and authorization
      across access tiers.
